    "media:imagetext": "-2-\nbreakfast. Then we got some box lunches and drove along the east\nand north coast of Kauai as far as the road goes.\nThe scenery is\nmagnificent - blue ocean, white breakers on alternate rocky shore\nand sand beaches. Some places the jungle covered mountains slant\nabruptly into the sea while elsewhere fertile valleys and gentle\nslopes are covered with rice-paddies or sugar cane.\nIt was a\n\"typical Lake George day\". We stopped for lunch on a beach where\nthe breakers came in too strong for amateur swimmers, but at one\nend a small rocky point jutted out to form a crystal clear pool\nwhere we all went in.\nWe got back to the Inn about 4:00 o'clock\nand at 5:00 Pompey called by to take us to their house for dinner\nand see the thee little girls. The latter are just as cute as\nthey can be with dispositions\nwell geared to the healthy, open-air\nlife they lead. The house is a most attractive one-storey ranch type,\nright on the ocean.\nWe had a perfectly delicious dinner with fried\nchicken, ice cream and coconut cake.\nAfter dinner Pompey showed us\nthe color film of his perents and Ruth and Steve taken and delivered\nby the Archie Lees.\nAlso pictures of the kids, the house and local\nshots including ones of the fish pond built by the Menehune or\nlegendary dwarfs who do all sorts of good and bad things on dark\nnights when everyone is asleep.\nPompey says that the Hawaiians\nexplain almost any shortcoming of their own es attributable to\nthe Menehunes, especially if anything in the home or office di-\nsappears.\nThe next day, Monday February 27th, we drove along the\nsouth shore and up to the Waimes Canyon which is a 4,000 foot deep\ncanyon second only to the Grand Canyon of Colorado in scenic\ngrandeur. It is much like the Grand Canyon except that the latter\nis done in technicolor while the Waimea is in various shades of\ngreen. The road there leads through fields of sugar cane and woods\nfilled with colored trees, bushes and flowers.\nThe little towns\nand small houses are picturesque and many typical of the real old\nHawaii. After late lunch at the Bakewells we flew back to Honolulu.\nHebe took some hula lessons as well es surfboard, with\nthe result that she used muscles never used before in the former\nand\ngot more bruises than a Notre Dame quarterback in the latter.\nShe and Debby loved all the Hawaiian music that was put on at every\none of the many parties given for the P.A.A. Directors.\nThey had\nthe best quartets and orchestras, end it meally was wonderful.\nThis morning Debby and I met Henry Cooper on his 7:00\na.m. arrival from San Francisco - Debby with a couple of leis and\nme with an aloha shirt.\nWe took him into the Royal Hawaiian for\nbreakfast and a look around before joining him on this flight.\nDave Ingalls had breekfast with us and then, while Debby and I\nfinished packing, drove Henry up to the Pali for a look et Honolulu.\nHenry is crazy about Honolulu, and I suspect some day will return\nfor a longer stay.\nLater - Henry and I have just come back from the cockpit\nwhere the Captain told him all about the navigation, engines, in-\nstruments, etc.\nThen we had a drink on the lower deck followed by\na steak dinner. He is now asleep, as is Debby. Hebe and I are about\nto join them.\nн.м.В.",
